Overview of Security as a Process
Security isn't a one-time setup—it's a continuous cycle that involves:
• 	Assessing requirements: Understanding what needs protection and identifying risks.
• 	Setting up systems: Implementing tools, policies, and infrastructure to safeguard assets.
• 	Hardening and monitoring: Strengthening systems against vulnerabilities and continuously watching for threats.
• 	Responding to attacks: Acting swiftly to contain and mitigate damage during security incidents.
• 	Deterring attackers: Using visible defenses and policies to discourage malicious activity.

🧠 Core Security Concepts
To contribute effectively to a security team, you should understand:
• 	Confidentiality: Ensuring sensitive data is accessible only to authorized users.
• 	Integrity: Protecting data from unauthorized changes.
• 	Availability: Making sure systems and data are accessible when needed.
• 	Risk Management: Identifying, evaluating, and mitigating potential threats.
• 	Defense in Depth: Using multiple layers of security controls to protect assets.
Non-repudiation is a critical concept in cybersecurity and digital communications that ensures a party in a transaction cannot deny the authenticity of their actions or commitments. It provides proof of origin, integrity, and delivery, making it essential for trust and accountability in digital systems.


